  "skeleton": {
    "about": "Applied to typed Latin and to the canonical romanisation alike: lowercase a-z only; a final h after a vowel dropped (dropTrailing: yeh, ye); the longest `replace` key at each position (else `letters`, else the letter itself); a symbol equal to the one before it is dropped; then every a between two consonants is dropped (dropBetweenConsonants). Urdu writes no short vowels, so a typed vowel can't say whether a letter stands for it: every vowel, and y and w (ی and و are vowels after a consonant and consonants elsewhere), is one symbol a, which survives only at the start or end of a word or next to another vowel. What is left is the consonants and where the word starts and ends with a vowel: is, us and as (اس) are as; kya, kiya (کیا) ka; mein, main (میں) man.",
